WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.350
Exercise two of this lecture

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:05.178
given the head of a singly linked list

00:00:05.178 --> 00:00:07.793
decide whether the list has a cycle

00:00:07.793 --> 00:00:08.430
that is

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.823
whether following next from the head ever comes

00:00:11.894 --> 00:00:14.580
back to a node it has already visited.

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:16.815
A cyclic list looks like

00:00:16.893 --> 00:00:18.543
the Greek letter rho.

00:00:18.543 --> 00:00:21.292
First a tail of t nodes, here two

00:00:21.292 --> 00:00:24.040
then a cycle of x nodes, here six.

00:00:24.119 --> 00:00:26.711
The last node of the cycle points

00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:29.852
back to the cycle's entrance, node two.

00:00:29.852 --> 00:00:32.837
An acyclic list ends in null instead

00:00:32.915 --> 00:00:35.900
and a circular list from the review is

00:00:35.978 --> 00:00:38.570
the case where the tail is empty.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:39.661
Why care?

00:00:39.743 --> 00:00:42.542
A broken pointer update can create

00:00:42.624 --> 00:00:44.682
such a cycle by accident

00:00:44.682 --> 00:00:47.399
and then every loop of the form

00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:50.280
while (p != null), runs forever.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.299
Method one: walk and hope.

00:00:53.299 --> 00:00:55.551
If the list is acyclic

00:00:55.551 --> 00:00:59.773
following next reaches null sooner or later.

00:00:59.773 --> 00:01:02.493
So walk at most limit steps

00:01:02.493 --> 00:01:06.340
and if no null turned up, answer cyclic.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.019
The trouble is choosing limit.

00:01:09.097 --> 00:01:12.357
It must be larger than the length of every

00:01:12.435 --> 00:01:14.919
acyclic list you might be given

00:01:14.919 --> 00:01:18.334
and you don't know that length; if you did

00:01:18.334 --> 00:01:20.042
you could just count.

00:01:20.042 --> 00:01:23.458
With too small a limit the answer is wrong

00:01:23.458 --> 00:01:24.778
with a huge one

00:01:24.778 --> 00:01:27.650
every cyclic list costs limit steps.

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:31.314
Method two: store every node you visit.

00:01:31.393 --> 00:01:33.290
If you ever reach a node

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:35.188
that is already stored

00:01:35.188 --> 00:01:37.481
you have gone round a cycle

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.011
the add method of a set returns

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.225
false in exactly that case.

00:01:42.225 --> 00:01:45.230
If you reach null, there is no cycle.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:47.360
Kept in a plain list

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.836
each search costs the size of that list

00:01:50.836 --> 00:01:54.142
so n nodes cost one plus two, up to n

00:01:54.142 --> 00:01:55.584
order n squared.

00:01:55.584 --> 00:01:58.805
In a hash set, from lecture eighteen

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:01.264
each search and insert takes

00:02:01.348 --> 00:02:03.298
constant expected time

00:02:03.298 --> 00:02:05.333
so the walk is order n

00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:08.300
but it needs order n extra memory.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.680
Here is method two on our list.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.751
Node zero is new, so it is added.

00:02:14.751 --> 00:02:18.274
Then one, two, three, four, five, six

00:02:18.274 --> 00:02:20.080
and seven, all new.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:23.144
Then next leads back to node two

00:02:23.144 --> 00:02:26.270
which is already in the set: a cycle.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.163
Method three uses two pointers

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.399
that both start at the head.

00:02:31.399 --> 00:02:34.869
The tortoise, slow, moves one node per step

00:02:34.869 --> 00:02:36.951
the hare, fast, moves two.

00:02:36.951 --> 00:02:40.344
After moving, if they are on the same node

00:02:40.344 --> 00:02:42.040
the list has a cycle.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:45.279
If fast, or the node after fast, is null

00:02:45.279 --> 00:02:48.209
the hare has found the end: no cycle.

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:51.370
This is Floyd's cycle-finding algorithm.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.058
On an acyclic list the hare finds the

00:02:55.148 --> 00:02:57.855
end in about n/2 steps.

00:02:57.855 --> 00:03:00.200
Now the interesting case.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.955
Both start at node zero.

00:03:02.955 --> 00:03:05.765
Step one: slow is at node one

00:03:05.765 --> 00:03:07.397
fast at node two.

00:03:07.397 --> 00:03:10.750
Step two: slow reaches the entrance

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:14.013
node two, and fast is at node four.

00:03:14.013 --> 00:03:16.460
Now both are on the cycle.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:18.291
Measure the gap

00:03:18.291 --> 00:03:21.253
how far the hare must still go

00:03:21.253 --> 00:03:23.567
forward round the cycle

00:03:23.567 --> 00:03:25.696
to reach the tortoise.

00:03:25.696 --> 00:03:27.270
Here it is four.

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:30.972
Step three: slow at three, fast at six

00:03:30.972 --> 00:03:31.990
gap three.

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:35.786
Step four: fast wraps round to node two

00:03:35.786 --> 00:03:36.619
gap two.

00:03:36.619 --> 00:03:38.470
Step five: gap one.

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:41.432
Step six: both are on node six.

00:03:41.432 --> 00:03:42.450
They meet.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.364
Each step the hare moves two

00:03:45.456 --> 00:03:47.929
nodes and the tortoise one

00:03:47.929 --> 00:03:51.134
so the gap shrinks by exactly one.

00:03:51.134 --> 00:03:54.522
It goes four, three, two, one, zero

00:03:54.614 --> 00:03:58.094
the hare cannot jump over the tortoise

00:03:58.185 --> 00:04:00.200
without landing on it.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:03.286
There is also a neat formula.

00:04:03.373 --> 00:04:06.570
After s steps the tortoise is at node

00:04:06.656 --> 00:04:09.163
s and the hare at node 2s.

00:04:09.163 --> 00:04:11.582
Once both are on the cycle

00:04:11.669 --> 00:04:15.730
they are on the same node exactly when the hare

00:04:15.817 --> 00:04:19.100
has gone a whole number of extra laps

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.434
2s minus s, which is s

00:04:21.434 --> 00:04:23.594
must be a multiple of x.

00:04:23.681 --> 00:04:26.619
So they first meet at the smallest

00:04:26.705 --> 00:04:29.557
multiple of x that is at least t.

00:04:29.557 --> 00:04:31.977
For tail two and cycle six

00:04:31.977 --> 00:04:33.532
that is step six.

00:04:33.618 --> 00:04:35.779
That is never more than t

00:04:35.865 --> 00:04:38.803
plus x minus one steps, at most n

00:04:38.803 --> 00:04:42.260
O(n) time, and constant extra space.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:44.764
The demo runs all three

00:04:44.848 --> 00:04:48.028
methods on five lists, and they agree.

00:04:48.028 --> 00:04:51.123
The meeting steps match the formula.

00:04:51.123 --> 00:04:54.470
The acyclic list: fast reaches the end

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:55.390
-1.

00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:57.984
Tail two, cycle six: step six.

00:04:57.984 --> 00:05:00.829
Tail zero, cycle five: step five.

00:05:00.829 --> 00:05:03.841
Tail seven, cycle three: step nine

00:05:03.925 --> 00:05:06.184
the first multiple of three

00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:08.192
that is at least seven.

00:05:08.192 --> 00:05:11.120
Tail three, cycle four: step four.

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:14.513
The last line shows method one failing

00:05:14.591 --> 00:05:17.009
a fifty-node list with no cycle

00:05:17.087 --> 00:05:18.804
is reported as cyclic

00:05:18.804 --> 00:05:20.910
because limit is only ten.

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:22.454
Now you try.

00:05:22.535 --> 00:05:25.026
A list has a tail of four nodes

00:05:25.106 --> 00:05:28.401
and a cycle of three, seven nodes in all.

00:05:28.482 --> 00:05:30.732
After how many steps do slow

00:05:30.812 --> 00:05:33.946
and fast first meet, and on which node

00:05:33.946 --> 00:05:36.518
counting from zero at the head?

00:05:36.518 --> 00:05:39.170
Pause the video and work it out.

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:45.278
The smallest multiple of three

00:05:45.362 --> 00:05:47.805
that is at least four is six

00:05:47.805 --> 00:05:50.332
so they meet after six steps

00:05:50.332 --> 00:05:52.270
with slow on node six.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:56.069
Check the hare: it has made twelve moves.

00:05:56.153 --> 00:05:58.508
Nodes zero to six take it to

00:05:58.592 --> 00:06:00.694
node six after six moves

00:06:00.694 --> 00:06:04.143
and the cycle four, five, six brings it

00:06:04.143 --> 00:06:06.666
six moves and two laps later

00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:08.180
back to node six.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:10.556
One trap from the slides.

00:06:10.628 --> 00:06:13.215
Their version tests whether A ==

00:06:13.286 --> 00:06:16.160
B at the top of the loop, before moving.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.819
But both pointers start at the head

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:20.687
so on the very first test

00:06:20.758 --> 00:06:22.411
they are already equal

00:06:22.483 --> 00:06:25.285
and the method answers cyclic for every

00:06:25.357 --> 00:06:27.440
list with at least two nodes.

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:32.279
On the acyclic list zero, one, two, three

00:06:32.279 --> 00:06:36.020
four, the slide version says true

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:38.691
and hasCycle says false.

00:06:38.691 --> 00:06:41.470
Move first, then compare.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:43.731
So, three methods.

00:06:43.731 --> 00:06:47.187
A walk with a limit needs no memory

00:06:47.187 --> 00:06:48.868
but can be wrong.

00:06:48.868 --> 00:06:51.763
A visited set is always right

00:06:51.763 --> 00:06:54.098
in linear expected time

00:06:54.098 --> 00:06:56.993
but needs linear extra memory.

00:06:56.993 --> 00:06:59.981
Floyd's method is always right

00:06:59.981 --> 00:07:03.810
in linear time and constant extra space.

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:05.966
The last exercise.

00:07:05.966 --> 00:07:09.119
Each node holds a lowercase letter.

00:07:09.207 --> 00:07:12.885
Remove every node whose letter has already

00:07:12.972 --> 00:07:15.512
appeared earlier in the list

00:07:15.512 --> 00:07:19.453
keeping the first occurrence of each letter.

00:07:19.453 --> 00:07:22.430
So a, b, b, a, c becomes a, b, c.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:25.721
Deleting a node from a singly linked list

00:07:25.793 --> 00:07:28.662
means making the node before it skip it

00:07:28.662 --> 00:07:31.387
pre.next becomes the node's next.

00:07:31.387 --> 00:07:34.041
So both methods keep a pointer, pre

00:07:34.041 --> 00:07:36.121
to the last node that stays.

00:07:36.121 --> 00:07:38.201
The first node always stays

00:07:38.273 --> 00:07:40.066
because its letter cannot

00:07:40.137 --> 00:07:41.644
have appeared before

00:07:41.644 --> 00:07:43.580
so the head never changes.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:46.291
Method one: for each node a

00:07:46.291 --> 00:07:48.878
walk the rest of the list with b

00:07:48.878 --> 00:07:50.628
and pre one behind it.

00:07:50.704 --> 00:07:52.607
Every later node with the

00:07:52.683 --> 00:07:54.509
same letter is unlinked

00:07:54.509 --> 00:07:56.563
and pre stays where it is

00:07:56.563 --> 00:07:59.607
because the node after pre has changed.

00:07:59.607 --> 00:08:01.814
Otherwise pre moves up to b.

00:08:01.814 --> 00:08:03.640
Either way, b moves on.

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:08.076
With M nodes the outer loop runs at most M times

00:08:08.161 --> 00:08:11.481
and the inner loop at most M - 1

00:08:11.481 --> 00:08:15.226
O(M²) time, constant extra space.

00:08:15.226 --> 00:08:18.376
The slides' version has three slips

00:08:18.376 --> 00:08:21.866
all fixed here: it skips the first node

00:08:21.866 --> 00:08:25.015
its inner loop stops one node early

00:08:25.015 --> 00:08:28.590
and it forgets to move b after an unlink.

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:31.996
Method two uses the fact that there are

00:08:32.069 --> 00:08:34.533
only 26 lowercase letters.

00:08:34.533 --> 00:08:37.576
Keep a table of 26 boolean flags

00:08:37.576 --> 00:08:39.315
all false at the start.

00:08:39.315 --> 00:08:41.852
a.data - 'a'

00:08:41.852 --> 00:08:44.098
turns a letter into its index

00:08:44.098 --> 00:08:45.620
0 to 25

00:08:45.692 --> 00:08:48.229
because char values are numbers and

00:08:48.301 --> 00:08:50.330
the letters are consecutive.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:53.252
A new letter is marked, and kept

00:08:53.252 --> 00:08:54.577
pre moves to it.

00:08:54.577 --> 00:08:57.305
A letter already seen is unlinked.

00:08:57.305 --> 00:09:00.267
One pass over the list: O(M) time

00:09:00.345 --> 00:09:02.917
and the extra space is 26

00:09:02.995 --> 00:09:05.567
flags whatever M is, so constant.

00:09:05.567 --> 00:09:07.671
This is direct addressing

00:09:07.749 --> 00:09:10.243
the simplest form of the hashing

00:09:10.321 --> 00:09:11.880
in lecture 18.

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:15.698
Trace it on a, b, b, a, c.

00:09:15.698 --> 00:09:19.060
a is new: its flag is set

00:09:19.060 --> 00:09:21.675
and pre moves to it.

00:09:21.675 --> 00:09:26.780
b is new: flag set, and pre moves to it.

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:29.644
The next b is already marked

00:09:29.644 --> 00:09:31.725
so pre.next skips it.

00:09:31.725 --> 00:09:33.546
Then a, also marked.

00:09:33.546 --> 00:09:35.800
Pre is still the first b

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:39.268
because the node after it was unlinked

00:09:39.268 --> 00:09:41.695
so its next now skips to c.

00:09:41.695 --> 00:09:43.950
c is new: flag set, kept

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:45.770
and pre moves to it.

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:48.499
The result is a, b, c

00:09:48.499 --> 00:09:53.040
the same as method one, in a single pass.

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:55.846
One pitfall from the slides

00:09:55.926 --> 00:09:58.550
they create the table as an array

00:09:58.629 --> 00:10:00.855
of Boolean with a capital B

00:10:00.855 --> 00:10:02.366
the wrapper class.

00:10:02.366 --> 00:10:05.626
A new Boolean array is filled with null

00:10:05.626 --> 00:10:06.500
not false.

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:09.442
So the test, b[0] == false

00:10:09.442 --> 00:10:10.953
has to unbox null

00:10:10.953 --> 00:10:13.736
and throws a NullPointerException.

00:10:13.736 --> 00:10:15.644
Use the primitive type

00:10:15.644 --> 00:10:17.552
boolean with a small b

00:10:17.552 --> 00:10:19.460
which starts all false.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:21.856
To sum up the lecture.

00:10:21.856 --> 00:10:25.322
One: linked lists store only what exists

00:10:25.405 --> 00:10:28.706
so sparse polynomials and matrices waste

00:10:28.788 --> 00:10:31.512
neither memory nor time on zeros.

00:10:31.512 --> 00:10:34.565
Two: two pointers solve list puzzles

00:10:34.648 --> 00:10:36.628
in constant extra space

00:10:36.628 --> 00:10:40.259
switching heads finds where two lists meet

00:10:40.259 --> 00:10:43.891
and a slow and a fast pointer find a cycle.

00:10:43.891 --> 00:10:44.468
Three

00:10:44.468 --> 00:10:47.769
a small table beats repeated searching

00:10:47.852 --> 00:10:50.080
26 flags turn O(M²)

00:10:50.163 --> 00:10:52.061
into O(M).

00:10:52.143 --> 00:10:55.527
And compare references with double equals

00:10:55.609 --> 00:10:57.920
when you mean the same node.

00:10:58.270 --> 00:10:59.826
Try exercise three

00:10:59.826 --> 00:11:02.120
where does the cycle start?

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:04.249
After slow and fast meet

00:11:04.249 --> 00:11:06.543
move slow back to the head

00:11:06.543 --> 00:11:10.065
and step both pointers one node at a time.

00:11:10.065 --> 00:11:13.750
Show that they meet at the cycle's entrance.

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:17.660
Next time, in lecture nine: stacks.

